    ["database_files",
    ".PVOC",
    "ProVoc Database File",
    "2.0",
    "A PVOC file is a ProVoc database created by iVocabulary, a macOS app used to manage and learn vocabulary words. It contains one or more libraries of vocabulary terms and definitions. PVOC files also store metadata, such as the author of the vocabulary collection and source and target languages of the terms. PVOC file open in Christian Beer iVocabulary 3 The PVOC file type was originally saved by ProVoc, an application developed by Arizona Software that preceded iVocabulary. However, ProVoc was eventually discontinued and now iVocabulary is the only program that creates PVOC files. While iVocabulary primarily saves vocabulary databases as IVOC files, you can export a database of vocabulary terms in ProVoc format by selecting File → Export → Export for ProVoc.. iVocabulary is utilized for various purposes, such as students studying for an exam or professionals learning new terms for their jobs. And iVocabulary users primarily save PVOC files for backing up vocabulary terms and for sharing terms with other iVocabulary users. You can only open PVOC files with the iVocabulary and ProVoc (discontinued) applications. To open a PVOC file with iVocabulary, select File → Open... Mac Christian Beer iVocabulary.",
    "Mac Christian Beer iVocabulary.",
    "?"],
    ["database_files",
    ".DAB",
    "OrangeCD Database File",
    "2.0",
    "A DAB file contains a database created by OrangeCD Catalog, a program included with the OrangeCD Suite used to catalog users digital music collections. It stores path locations to imported media files, such as.MP3,.OGG, and.WMA files, and includes metadata, such as song titles, artists, year released, label, and genre. DAB file open in OrangeCD Catalog 6.5 OrangeCD Catalog is designed to make it easier for you to organize your locally stored music in one place. The application allows you to add various media from CDs, DVDs, and digital audio files into a database, and stores references to the locations of the media in the DAB database file. The software also references media metadata stored in the database file. This allows you to quickly search through your collection by various tags, such as artist, title, the year released, and genre. You can create a new database with OrangeCD Catalog by selecting Database → New database.. DAB files are saved in your Music folder, by default. You can also manage the database by selecting Database → Manage database. Sample Database.dab - The default name given to a new database by OrangeCD Catalog. You can only open DAB files with OrangeCD Catalog. To open a DAB file, select Database → Open database... Windows OrangeCD Suite.",
    "Windows OrangeCD Suite.",
    "?"],
    ["database_files",
    ".SDA",
    "StarMoney User Database Archive",
    "2.0",
    "Drawing or diagram created by Apache OpenOffice Draw, a vector graphics drawing program included with Apache OpenOffice and Oracles Open Office; typically contains drawn vector objects but also supports raster graphics. SDA files can be used for storing organizational charts, flowcharts, network diagrams, and other types of drawings. NOTE: SDA files were used by StarOffice through version 5. StarOffice 6 adopted the.SXD format, but Apache OpenOffice and Open Office now both use.ODG files for drawings. Apache OpenOffice was previously known as OpenOffice.org. Windows OpenOffice Draw. LibreOffice. Mac OpenOffice Draw. Planamesa NeoOffice. LibreOffice. Linux OpenOffice Draw. LibreOfficeFree. An SDA file is a database archive created by the Windows version of Star Finanz StarMoney, a personal and business banking application. It contains a user database generated from a users.SDY and.SDX files, and includes account information, transactions, contacts, and other user data. SDA files are created to archive and back up databases. StarMoney is developed by Star Finanz, a German-based company. The application is available in personal and business versions, which both may create SDA files. You can choose whether or not StarMoney creates SDA archives. To do so, select Settings → General → Archiving, then select Activate Archiving under Archiving Settings. StarMoney generates SDA archives in the following directory, by default (beginning with StarMoney 7.0): C:/​ProgramData/​StarMoney [version]/​Profile The ProgramData folder is hidden by default. You can set hidden files and folders to be shown in the Windows File Explorer utility by following our Windows 8 and Windows 10 video tutorials. You can only open SDA archives with the Windows version of Star Finanz StarMoney. Windows Star Finanz StarMoney.",
    "Windows OpenOffice Draw. LibreOffice. Mac OpenOffice Draw. Planamesa NeoOffice. LibreOffice. Linux OpenOffice Draw. LibreOffice. Windows Star Finanz StarMoney.",
    "?"],
    ["database_files",
    ".TVDB",
    "TreeView Database",
    "2.0",
    "A TVDB file is a database file used by Apple TV. It is stored within TV Library, which is the file that contains your Apple TV library. TVDB files contain information about either your Apple TV settings or your Apple TV library. You are most likely to encounter a TVDB file after right-clicking TV Library and selecting Show Package Contents. Each TV Library file contains the following three TVDB files: Application.tvbd: Stores Apple TV preferences and settings Library Preferences.tvdb: Stores library preferences and settings Library.tvdb: Stores information about each video in your Apple TV library By default, TV Library is stored at Macintosh HD/​Users/​YourUserName/​Movies/​TV on Macs. TVDB files are not meant to be opened or edited. Editing a TVDB file may affect Apple TVs ability to load and show your library. Mac Apple TV Included with OS. iOS Apple TV Included with OS. A TVDB file is a genealogy database created by Genealogy Supplies TreeView. It contains a record of a persons family tree, including names, birthdates, death dates, and relationships for each included person (if applicable). It may also include additional notes and details for each person. TreeView is a genealogy program that allows a user to chart their (or someone elses) family tree. To do so, users can either import a tree saved in the GEDCOM.GED format or create a tree from scratch. When a user saves their tree in TreeView, regardless of whether they imported the tree or created it from scratch, it is saved as a TVDB file. After opening a TVDB file in TreeView, users can view the data the file contains in many ways. For example, users can view the tree in a variety of formats, including a pedigree, family tree, hourglass, relationship, or fan format. Users can also add a background and photographs to their tree, before printing the tree (or a subset of the data it contains) as a physical chart. NOTE: Users can also sync their TVDB files to their TreeView online account, to access the files over the Internet or in the TreeView mobile apps. You can open a TVDB file with Genealogy Supplies TreeView (Windows, Mac). To do so, select File → Open. Then, navigate to and open your TVDB file. Mac Genealogy Supplies TreeView. Windows Genealogy Supplies TreeView.",
    "Mac Apple TV Included with OS. iOS Apple TV Included with OS. Mac Genealogy Supplies TreeView. Windows Genealogy Supplies TreeView.",
    "?"],
    ["database_files",
    ".OWC",
    "OutWit Catch Database",
    "2.0",
    "Search database or collection basket generated by Outwit, an application for extracting and organizing data from online sources; used during the Web harvesting process when content is gathered by Outwit. Windows Outwit Hub. Mozilla Firefox with OutWit Hub add-on. Mac Outwit Hub. Mozilla Firefox with OutWit Hub add-on. Linux Outwit Hub. Mozilla Firefox with OutWit Hub add-onFree.",
    "Windows Outwit Hub. Mozilla Firefox with OutWit Hub add-on. Mac Outwit Hub. Mozilla Firefox with OutWit Hub add-on. Linux Outwit Hub. Mozilla Firefox with OutWit Hub add-on.",
    "?"],
    ["database_files",
    ".MUSICDB",
    "Apple Music Library",
    "2.0",
    "A MUSICDB file is a database used by Apple Music, a free audio player bundled with macOS. It contains information about songs stored in the Music library or library preferences or application settings for Music. MUSICDB files are packaged with the.MUSICLIBRARY file. Apple Music is an audio player that replaced iTunes when Apple released macOS Catalina (version 10.15) in October 2019. The MUSICDB file is similar to the.ITDB database file used by iTunes to store song information. You can find MUSICDB files in the Music Library macOS package) located in the following location: /​Users/​username/​Music/​Music/​Music Library macOS does not display the.musiclibrary file extension for the Music Library package. However, if you right-click the file and select Get Info, you can verify the.musiclibrary file extension in the Name & Extension field. You can open a MUSICLIBRARY package to explore its contents by right-clicking the file and selecting Show Package Contents. Typical contents include the following files: Application.musicdb Extras.itdb Genius.itdb Library Preferences.musicdb Library.musicdb Preferences.plist. MUSICDB files are not meant to be opened. Instead, they store information referenced by Apple Music to organize and play music. Mac Apple Music Included with OS.",
    "Mac Apple Music Included with OS.",
    "?"],
    ["database_files",
    ".ODL",
    "OneDrive Event Log",
    "2.0",
    "File written in the Object Description Language (ODL), a language used in the Windows software programming context; allows developers to make their ActiveX objects visible to other vendors by exposing and publishing properties of the objects. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. An ODL file is an event log created by the Windows or Mac version of Microsoft OneDrive. It contains a list of events that occurred while a user was using OneDrive. OneDrive uses ODL files to record actions synced to a users OneDrive account. Microsoft OneDrive is a file hosting and sharing app used by individuals, organizations, and businesses to share documents, photos, and other files with each other. While users primarily access OneDrive via a web browser, they can also download desktop versions of OneDrive, which allow them to access and edit files offline. The desktop versions of OneDrive store a record of users actions in ODL files. Microsoft can use these files to troubleshoot any synchronization issues that occur after a user edits files offline. Only a users most recent OneDrive log is stored as an ODL file. When OneDrive creates a new ODL file, it compresses the previous ODL file and renames it to use the.ODLGZ extension. ODL files are stored in the following locations by default: Windows: C:/​Users/​[USER]/​AppData/​Local/​Microsoft/​OneDrive/​logs Mac: ~/​Users/​[USER]/​Library/​Logs/​OneDrive. While ODL files are not meant to be opened, you can view the contents of your ODL files using GitHub user ydkhatris OneDrive Log ODL Reader (multiplatform). Windows Microsoft OneDrive. OneDrive Log ODL Reader. Mac Microsoft OneDrive. OneDrive Log ODL ReaderFree.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Windows Microsoft OneDrive. OneDrive Log ODL Reader. Mac Microsoft OneDrive. OneDrive Log ODL Reader.",
    "?"],
    ["database_files",
    ".ICDB",
    "Icaros Thumbnail Database",
    "2.0",
    "An ICDB file is a thumbnail database created by Icaros, a program used to show thumbnail images for videos in Windows File Explorer. It contains cached thumbnails. Icaros uses ICDB files to load thumbnails if they are not present in File Explorers thumbnail cache. Shark007 Icaros uses a set of Windows shell extensions to provide thumbnails for many types of videos. Icaros can provide thumbnails for videos saved in most formats recognized by FFmpeg. Optionally, a user can choose to cache the thumbnails Icaros creates in an ICDB file. To enable caching, users must select Cache from Icaross menu. They can then choose to manually or automatically create ICDB files. If Windows File Explorer deletes a thumbnail Icaros created, Icaros can retrieve the thumbnail from its cache, speeding up the process of regenerating that thumbnail. Typically, Icaros ICDB files are saved in the following directory: C:/​Users/​UserName/​AppData/​Local/​Icaros/​IcarosCache. ICDB files are not meant to be opened. Shark007 Icaros (Windows) uses ICDB files to load cached thumbnail images. Windows Shark007 IcarosFree.",
    "Windows Shark007 Icaros.",
    "?"],
    ["database_files",
    ".FOL",
    "PFS First Choice Database File",
    "1.6",
    "Database file created by Personal Filing System (PFS) First Choice, a DOS-based program developed during early versions of Windows to create databases with flat files; contains data stored as a collection of fields with their types and values; used to store and query various types of data as well as create reports. FOL files are extremely rare and are no longer supported. They may be converted into usable data formats using FirstOut or first2html. PFS First Choice considered abandonware. Windows PFS First Choice Advanced Computer Innovations FirstOut first2html Linux first2html.",
    "Windows PFS First Choice Advanced Computer Innovations FirstOut first2html. Linux first2html.",
    "?"],
